SRC/slice -- slice, as built for OS-9/68000===========================================slice splits a file into pieces: at every line matching a pattern, orevery n lines, into output files named by format strings.  GaryPuckering, 1987, from R. E. Quin's mailsplit; comp.sources.unix volume13, issue 73.  ORIG holds the posting's five files unchanged.Build: slice.c and recomp.c, linked with os9lib (tools/rebuild recipe`slice').What changed, every change inside #ifdef OSK:  1. slice.c: <strings.h> for <string.h>; no <sys/file.h>; F_OK is 0,     which asks access() only whether the file exists; strtok() and     strpbrk() are declared, because an undeclared function returning a     pointer is taken as returning int.  2. slice.c and opts.h: the bit bucket is /nil, not /dev/null -- the     default reject file, the `@' output name and their messages.  3. opts.h: an RBF file name holds only letters, digits, `.', `_' and     `$', so the default output name is `<input>.001' rather than     `<input>:001', and -m's mailbox name is `<box>.<year>.<mm>.<dd>':     the posted format ended in the message's hh:mm:ss, which no RBF     name can carry, so one day's messages append to one file, in the     order the mailbox had them.  4. recomp.c is new, written for this collection: the BSD re_comp() and     re_exec() that slice calls, over Henry Spencer's regcomp() and     regexec() in os9lib, which this C library otherwise lacks.     Patterns are Spencer's syntax, which for slice's uses is ed's.     regexp.h is os9lib's own, copied from SRC/top_os9lib.     re_exec() matches the line without its end-of-line character, as     ed and BSD saw a line, so a pattern ending in `$' works.As posted, and left alone: with -n, the first n-1 lines go to the rejectfile, and every slice after that starts on an n'th line.  The manualstill says /dev/null; on OS-9 read /nil.    slice -f mail.box -m              one file per day, in date order    slice -f notes -x '^--' part#n    part1, part2 ... cut lines dropped